        <body>
            <div xml:id="d145-a1">

                <pb facs="0344" n="254" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg254"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No.
                    23.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Prince Kung to Mr.
                            Avery</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date
                            ana="#date_apparent-typo-based-on-document-content"
                            calendar="chinese-era chinese-lunisolar                         gregorian"
                            when="1875-01-13"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tungchi</hi>, 12<hi
                                rendition="#italic">th year</hi>, 12<hi rendition="#italic">th
                                moon</hi>, 6<hi rendition="#italic">th day</hi>. (January 13,
                            1865.)</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Prince Kung, chief secretary of state for foreign affairs, herewith makes a
                    communication.</p>
                <pb facs="0345" n="255" xml:id="pg255"/>
                <p>I have reverently to inform you that yesterday His Majesty took the long journey
                    on the dragon-chariot, to become a guest on high. I and all the imperial kindred
                    are on this account filled with a grief and anguish too great for expression;
                    and, moreover, we are at this moment wholly taken up with the reverent
                    preparation of the rites connected with his obsequies; things of the greatest
                    importance, and very perplexing.</p>
                <p>Whatever matters may now be under discussion with your excellency, or may come up
                    in the ordinary course of affairs, unless they are of imperative necessity, must
                    be for a while deferred, until we can resume their discussion and
                    settlement.</p>
                <p>I have therefore at this time only to inform your excellency; which, indeed, is
                    the object of this communication.</p>
                <p>To His Excellency <hi rendition="#small-caps">Benjamin P. Avery</hi>,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">United States Minister to China.</hi></p>
            </div>
        </body>
